'''abreast''' (a-brest′), ''prep. phr.'' as ''adv.'' or ''a.'' [< ''a''<sup>3</sup>, ''prep.'', on, + ''breast.''] '''1.''' Side by side, with breasts in a line: as, “the riders rode ''abreast,''” ''Dryden.''
{{smaller block|It [the wall of Chester] has everywhere, however, a rugged outer parapet and a broad hollow flagging, wide enough for two strollers ''abreast.''
{{right|''H. James, Jr.'', Trans. Sketches, p. 9.}}}}
'''2.''' ''Naut.'': (''a'') Lying or moving side by side, with stems equally advanced. (''b'') When used to indicate the situation of a vessel in regard to another object, opposite; over against; lying so that the object is on a line with the beam: in this sense with ''of.''
{{smaller block|The Bellona . . . kept too close to the starboard shoal, and grounded ''abreast of'' the outer ship of the enemy.
{{right|''Southey'', Nelson, II. 121.}}}}
'''3.''' Figuratively, up to the same pitch or level: used with ''of'' or ''with'': as, to keep ''abreast of'' the times in science, etc.—'''4†.''' At the same time; simultaneously.
{{smaller block|''Abreast'' therewith began a convocation. {{right|''Fuller.''}}}}

'''Line abreast''', {{smaller|a formation of a squadron in which the ships are abreast of one another.}}

'''abrede†''', ''prep. phr.'' as ''adv.'' A Middle English form of ''abread. Rom. of the Rose.''

'''abregge†''', ''v. t.'' A Middle English form of ''abridge. Chaucer.''

'''abrenounce†''' (ab-rē-nouns′), ''v. t.'' [< L. ''ab'', from (here intensive), + E. ''renounce'', after LL. ''abrenuntiāre'', < L. ''ab'' + ''renuntiāre'', renounce: see ''renounce.''] To renounce absolutely.
{{smaller block|Under pain of the pope’s curse . . . either to ''abrenounce'' their wives or their livings.
{{right|''Foxe'', Book of Martyrs, Acts and Deeds, fol. 159.}}}}

readings.c4s3:
by condensation or omission, or both; rewrite or reconstruct on a smaller scale; put the main or essential parts of into less space: used of writings: as, Justin ''abridged'' the history of Trogus Pompeius.
{{smaller block|The antiquities of Richborough and Reculver, ''abridged'' from the Latin of Mr. Archdeacon Battely.
{{right|''N. and Q.'', 6th ser., X. 143.}}}}
'''3.''' To lessen; diminish: as, to ''abridge'' labor.
{{smaller block|Power controlled or ''abridged'' is almost always the rival and enemy of that power by which it is controlled or ''abridged.''
{{right|''A. Hamilton'', Federalist, No. 15.}}}}
'''4.''' To deprive; cut off: followed by ''of'', and formerly also by ''from:'' as, to ''abridge'' one ''of'' rights or enjoyments.
{{smaller block|<poem>Nor do I now make moan to be ''abridg’d''
''From'' such a noble rate.</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', M. of V., i. 1.}}}}
'''5.''' In ''alg.'', to reduce, as a compound quantity or equation, to a more simple form. = '''Syn. 2.''' {{smaller|To cut down, prune. See ''abbreviate.''—'''4.''' To dispossess, divest, strip, despoil.}}

'''abridgedly''' (a-brij′ed-li), ''adv.'' In a concise or shortened form.

'''abridger''' (a-brij′ėr), ''n.'' One who or that which abridges, by curtailing, shortening, or condensing.
{{smaller block|Criticks have been represented as the great ''abridgers'' of the native liberty of genius.
{{right|''H. Blair'', Lectures, iii.}}}}
{{smaller block|''Abridgers'' are a kind of literary men to whom the indolence of modern readers . . . give[s] ample employment.
{{right|''I. D’Israeli'', Curios. of Lit., II. 67.}}}}
